The '''Kamakã language''' (Kamakan), or '''Ezeshio''', is an extinct language of a [[Kamakã languages|small family]], belonging to the [[Macro-Jê languages]] of [[Brazil]].<ref name="Nikulin-Macro-Je">Nikulin, Andrey. 2020. ''[http://etnolinguistica.org/local--files/tese%3Anikulin-2020/Nikulin_2020_Proto-Macro-Je.pdf Proto-Macro-Jê: um estudo reconstrutivo]''. Doctoral dissertation, University of Brasília.</ref> [[Kotoxó language|Kotoxó]] and Mongoyó/Mangaló are sometimes included as dialects.

== Classification ==
Kamakã is a [[Macro-Jê languages|Macro-Jê language]]. It was spoken by several groups of indigenous peoples who lived in [[Bahia]], including the Kamakã, Mongoyó, Menién, Kotoxó and Masakará.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Ribeiro |first=Eduardo Rivail |date=October 2010 |title=Nimuendajú was right: The inclusion of the jabutí language family in the Macro-Jê stock |url=https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/658056 |journal=International Journal of American Linguistics |language=en |volume=76 |issue=4 |pages=547 |doi=10.1086/658056 |issn=0020-7071}}</ref>

== History ==
The {{ill|Kamakã people|pt|Camacãs}} are now completely integrated into the [[Pataxó-hã-hã-hãe]] people.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Nelson |first=Jessica F. |url=https://repository.arizona.edu/bitstream/handle/10150/630222/azu_etd_16653_sip1_m.pdf |title=Pataxó Hãhãhãe: Race, Indigeneity and Language Revitalization in the Brazilian Northeast |publisher=University of Arizona |year=2018}}</ref>

